{"type": "FeatureCollection", "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Central Ohio Railroad", "line": "Central Ohio: Bellaire \u2013 Columbus", "id": "co-bellaire-columbus", "color": "#00447C", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 35, "speed_source": "estimated: 1850s-era steam service; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1854, "end": 1866, "headway_min": 480, "note": "Chartered Feb 1847 by Zanesville interests. Construction was difficult - crossing the Muskingum River at Zanesville, cutting 700 feet through sandstone at Blackhand Gorge along the Licking River, and heavy fill/trestle work along Big Walnut Creek. Opened Zanesville-Newark Jan 19, 1852, Newark-Columbus a year later, and the full 137-mile Bellaire-Columbus line in November 1854. Bellaire sat directly across the Ohio River from the B&O's Benwood station (near Wheeling), giving an immediate connection to the B&O main stem. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Ohio_Railroad"]}, {"start": 1866, "end": 1971, "headway_min": 180, "note": "The B&O, which had already taken a financial interest in the CO to keep it running during the Civil War, leased the railroad in 1866 and began a capital improvement program - extending the B&O's own network from Wheeling on to Columbus. Passenger service wound down through the mid-20th century like the rest of the B&O system, ending with Amtrak's creation May 1, 1971. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Ohio_Railroad"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-82.39699366317981, 40.05800742603389], "to": [-83.0007065, 39.9622601], "chord_mi": 32.63, "traced_mi": 33.94, "snap_m": [9, 641], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["C&N Subdivision (30.7 mi)", "Columbus and Ohio River Railroad (1.1 mi)", "joint (B&O/PRR) (1.08 mi)", "Dayton District (0.8 mi)", "Mt. Vernon Subdivision (0.11 mi)", "(rail) (0.1 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-81.59557959396146, 40.02439988576788], "to": [-82.01032022855338, 39.94149016031903], "chord_mi": 22.68, "traced_mi": 27.95, "snap_m": [11, 16], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["B&O (27.22 mi)", "PRR [abandoned] (0.65 mi)", "Columbus and Ohio River Railroad (0.08 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}], "geometry_quality": "Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 1 ruled segment totalling 33 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed). Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 2 ruled segments totalling 55 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed)."}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-80.75077606848075, 40.011267595932445], [-80.75774146184251, 40.00920755910547], [-80.79520445271685, 40.02168806082608], [-80.8042449687437, 40.01791202694914], [-80.81791768583238, 40.02529680791179], [-80.83854183912067, 40.02140167084183], [-80.86923659403661, 40.00377062509644], [-80.88497645974704, 40.002652498611525], [-80.90526452667348, 40.0159771377375], [-80.91826524660912, 40.013848048518504], [-80.93666824701427, 40.01734583540553], [-80.9521800660048, 40.03036151746687], [-80.9783275264516, 40.022026393530766], [-81.02342696643744, 40.03085296080078], [-81.0470112552069, 40.02683189930708], [-81.10933881294304, 40.0